#
# Token recognition where ksh93 reads the input differently from every other
# shell on the panel. core/tokenization.tests carries the questions the four
# answer alike; these are the ones it could not.

# A backslash-newline inside an unquoted word inside $( … ). bash, zsh and
# dash treat it as a line continuation and join the two lines; ksh93 keeps the
# backslash and the newline as characters of the word. It is narrower than it
# looks — the same spelling after a blank, inside double quotes, or inside
# backticks joins in every shell, and so does the top-level form. See #2869,
# which this case is the record of.
#
# These rows are expected to disagree, and #2869 says why they are not being
# fixed: ksh93's answer is not a function of the word. Measured 2026-09-16
# against ksh93u+ 2012-08-01, three identical words in one file, differing
# only in what stands in front of them in their own command:
#
#     f() { printf '<%s>' "$@"; }
#     $(f x ab\⏎cd)           <x><ab\⏎cd>       kept
#     $(f x x ab\⏎cd)         <x><x><abcd>      joined
#     $(ffffffff x x ab\⏎cd)  <x><x><ab\⏎cd>    kept
#
# So whether the two characters survive depends on the count, the widths and
# the quoting of the earlier words of the command — lexer state, with no
# grammar rule behind it that a dialect flag could name. Rows 6 and 7 below
# are that measurement, executable: the same word one argument apart. A
# first-order reading — "one to four name characters in front of it, nothing
# but kept continuations since, and the word not reached across a
# continuation" — fits every row here and is still false, because it says
# row 7 keeps. Do not implement it to make this file strict.
printf '1[%s]\n' "$(printf '%s' ab\
cd)"
printf '2[%s]\n' "$(printf '%s' ab\
)"
printf '3[%s]\n' "$(printf '%s' "ab\
cd")"
printf '4[%s]\n' "$(printf '%s%s' ab \
cd)"
printf '5[%s]\n' "$( printf '%s' ab\
cd )"
f() { printf '<%s>' "$@"; }
printf '6[%s]\n' "$(f x ab\
cd)"
printf '7[%s]\n' "$(f x x ab\
cd)"

# A hex escape in $'...'. bash and zsh stop after two digits, so $'\x414' is
# the two characters A and 4; ksh93 keeps taking hex digits.
printf '[%s]\n' $'\x414'
v=$'\x414'
printf 'length: %s\n' "${#v}"
